{
    "title": "Restricting and Limiting the Entry of Foreign Nationals To Protect the Security of the United States",
    "type": "Presidential Document",
    "date": "December 19, 2025",
    "url": "https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-19/pdf/2025-23570.pdf",
    "summary": "The White House issued a proclamation that continues and expands travel and visa restrictions on nationals from a broad list of countries, citing gaps in identity checks, document reliability, crime and terror risks, and high visa overstay rates. The rules take effect January 1, 2026, and will be reviewed every six months.\n\nIn short, earlier limits remain in place, and new ones are added. Some countries face a full suspension of entry for both immigrants and visitors, including several in Africa and the Middle East, as well as people traveling on documents issued by the Palestinian Authority. Others face partial limits that primarily affect tourist, business, and student visas, and in some cases immigrant visas too. One country, Turkmenistan, sees some easing for temporary visitors but keeps a block on immigration. The policy also narrows broad family-based exceptions, saying those pathways have been exploited in the past and are hard to verify in countries with weak records.\n\nThe administration says the goal is to prevent threats before they reach U.S. borders and to press foreign governments to improve their identity systems, share information, and take back their nationals when ordered removed. It points to corruption, unreliable civil records, limited government control over territory, and citizenship-by-investment programs that can mask identities as key concerns. Existing visas are not revoked, and people can still seek asylum or refugee protection. There are exceptions for green card holders, diplomats, some athletes for major events, certain U.S. government special immigrants, and case-by-case national interest waivers.\n\nWho stands to benefit are U.S. security and law enforcement agencies that want tighter screening, as well as policymakers and voters who prioritize a more cautious approach to admissions from countries with weak documentation. The policy could also give Washington leverage to push for reforms abroad.\n\nThose most affected are travelers, students, workers, and family members from the named countries, including U.S.-based communities hoping to reunite with relatives. U.S. universities, hospitals, and employers that rely on talent and visitors from these regions may feel the impact, and bilateral relations with some governments could be strained. Supporters will see this as prudent risk management; critics will view it as overly broad and burdensome for many who pose no threat.",
